This version uses a local reference named `newHead` to make the change clear. It points to the second node, or to `null` when the list has zero or one node.

The original `head` variable is not passed by reference in this template, so returning `newHead` is how the caller receives the changed list. An empty input and a single-node input are both covered.

```csharp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    public int data;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode() { data = 0; next = null; }
    public ListNode(int x) { data = x; next = null; }
    public ListNode(int x, ListNode next) { data = x; this.next = next; }
}
*/
class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteHead(ListNode head) {
        ListNode newHead = head == null ? null : head.next;
        return newHead;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The removed node is no longer reachable from the returned head.

The reference parameter lets the method update `head` directly. When a head exists, assigning `head->next` skips the first node. The same updated pointer is returned for the platform result.

Dry run: for `4 -> 8 -> 12`, `head` first points to `4`. After the assignment it points to `8`, giving `8 -> 12`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{

  public:
    int data;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode() : data(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : data(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : data(x), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteHead(ListNode* &head) {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        head = head->next;
        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The first node is removed from the reachable list, and the input head reference is changed.

The stopping case handles an empty list. Otherwise, the next node is returned as the new starting node. The small recursive helper is only used to keep the empty-list check separate from the pointer update.

For a list with one node, the returned `null` means that the list becomes empty. No traversal is needed, so the recursion stops immediately.

```javascript
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    constructor(x, next){
        this.data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/
class Solution {
    deleteHead(head) {
        return this.removeFirst(head);
    }

    removeFirst(node) {
        if (node === null || node === undefined) {
            return null;
        }
        return node.next;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, since the helper makes only one call and does not visit later nodes.

The head is the first node, so removing it only means returning `head.next`. If the list is empty, the head is already `null`.

The original list is not changed inside this Java method because the new head is returned to the caller.

```java
/*
Defination of linked list
class ListNode {
    int data;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        this.data = 0;
        this.next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int x) {
        this.data = x;
        this.next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) {
        this.data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/
class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteHead(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }
        return head.next;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(1)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

